## What it does

- `xmpl100::MovingAverage<N>`: the mean of the last samples in fixed storage (the template's example utility).
- One include root, `@ROOT@/`, and one CMake target, `xmpl100::xmpl100`.
- Every fallible call returns `Result<T>` with an `Error`: no exceptions, no heap, no RTTI.
- MCU-agnostic: the same code on any MCU and on the host (`examples/host`).

## Add it to your project

Pick the row that matches your build; each is a few steps, and [Integration](docs/integration.md) has the
details.

<!-- scaffold:readme-picker -->
| Your build | Steps below |
| --- | --- |
| Plain CMake: STM32 (CubeMX), RP2040 (Pico SDK), nRF Connect, a host app | [A](#a-cmake-any-mcu) |
| ESP-IDF | [B](#b-esp-idf) |
| An SDK or OS image that installs libraries | [C](#c-installed-package) |
<!-- /scaffold:readme-picker -->

### A. CMake, any MCU

1. Add the library as a submodule, pinned to the version you chose:

   ```bash
   git submodule add https://github.com/@ORG@/@REPO@.git external/@REPO@
   ```

2. In your `CMakeLists.txt`, after `project()`:

   ```cmake
   add_subdirectory(external/@REPO@)
   target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE xmpl100::xmpl100)   # brings the headers and C++20
   ```

3. In your code, `#include "@ROOT@/MovingAverage.h"` and build. A first build
   that compiles the quickstart above is the check that the library is wired in.
<!-- scaffold:platform-esp32 -->

### B. ESP-IDF

1. Add the library under your project's `components/`; ESP-IDF finds it there and names it after the folder:

   ```bash
   git submodule add https://github.com/@ORG@/@REPO@.git components/xmpl100
   ```

2. In `main/CMakeLists.txt`, list it: `idf_component_register(SRCS "main.cpp" REQUIRES xmpl100)`.
3. `idf.py menuconfig` now has a **@TITLE@** menu (the moving average window),
   and `idf.py build` builds the library with your app. [`examples/esp32`](examples/esp32) is a complete
   project.
<!-- /scaffold:platform-esp32 -->

### C. Installed package

1. Install it once into a prefix:

   ```bash
   cmake -S external/@REPO@ -B build-xmpl100 && cmake --install build-xmpl100 --prefix /opt/sdk
   ```

2. In your project, with `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/sdk`:

   ```cmake
   find_package(xmpl100 0.1 REQUIRED)
   target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE xmpl100::xmpl100)
   ```

The target is `xmpl100::xmpl100` every way, so switching later changes nothing else.

